//! PdfCraft patch (104): the 750 characters used most often in Taiwan's laws and regulations,
//! for [`super::cjk_order`]'s scoring (data, not code: see `ATTRIBUTION.toml`).
//!
//! Source: the Ministry of Justice (Taiwan) national laws and regulations database, Chinese
//! laws data file and Chinese regulations data file (data.gov.tw datasets 18289 and 18290),
//! updated 2026-10-30 (`ChLaw_CF.zip`, SHA-256
//! `bf710bb9836b7a5e717799660bb5155d95eebec87f78fc0adcaec25fc33dfa53`; `MingLing.zip`, SHA-256
//! `37941aa051213457abf5fcf4c0a5b7a45fffe47e09dbfa341c99e49e9401fd0c`), released under the Open
//! Government Data License, version 1.0, used here under CC BY 4.0 as its section 4.2 permits
//! (attribution in `NOTICE`).
//!
//! How the list is made from the two files, so it can be checked or remade from a later
//! snapshot (the source files are not in the repository):
//!
//! 1. Read `FalV.xml` from `ChLaw_CF.zip` and `MingLing.xml` from `MingLing.zip` as UTF-8 (a
//!    byte-order mark dropped).
//! 2. In each, replace every `<![CDATA[` and `]]>` with a space, then every tag (`<`, anything
//!    but `>`, `>`) with a space: what remains is the text of every text node.
//! 3. Count each character at U+3000 or above, other than U+FFFD, over both files together.
//! 4. Rank by count, most first, equal counts by code point, and keep the first 750.
//! 5. Write their code points here in code point order.
//!
//! The list holds 738 ideographs and 12 full-width punctuation marks and symbols (`、`, `，`,
//! `。`, `「`, `」`, `（`, `）`, `：`, `；`, `～`, `＞` and the ideographic space); every one has
//! a Big5 code. Together they make up 96.1% of the characters of the two files.

